# Upstream adds the reheat's thrust the step the lever passes military power,
# whatever the core is doing: an F-16 slammed from idle had its full 91 kN at
# 63 % core speed, the turbine still spooling, while the engine's own fuel flow
# rose toward the reheat's at the augmented rate it has (Seek at 5,000 lb/h
# a second). Now the reheat lights only with the core at military speed (the
# 97 % augmethod 1 gates on), and it is lit as far as that fuel has risen: its
# share of the way from the dry engine's flow to full reheat's, from the
# engine's own TSFC, ATSFC and thrust tables - so a larger reheat takes longer
# to light, and it goes out at the rate the fuel falls. The thrust adds that
# share of the reheat's, and the nozzle is opened by it (its area scheduled
# with the reheat's fuel), which flightsim reads as the reheat lit. Nothing
# changes for an engine whose lever stays at or below military power.
